    "title": "Huntsville residents see second electrical rate hike this year - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Carolyn Kimball ,\n8 hours ago\nHUNTSVILLE, Ala. (WHNT) – Huntsville residents may notice their electricity bill is higher this month after the second installment of a rate increase took effect Oct. 1.\nThe Huntsville City Council approved the 5.1% hike last year, with Huntsville Utilities citing rising costs of equipment and operation.\nSpecial Olympics of Madison County needs volunteers for 56th track and field games “Every person in the United States has seen the cost of everything increase,” said Spokesperson Joe Gehrdes. “That affects utilities too.”\nHuntsville Utilities rolled out the first installment in January, raising the Residential Consumption Charge 3.9%. Starting this month, residential customers will pay 1.2% more.\nSome residents were also notified of an additional $5.55 charge for using services over 200-amp.\n“If you get 400 amp service, that’s roughly the same as a small commercial customer,” said Gehrdes, “and so that translates into more robust hardware required to provide that service.”\nOnly about 10% of Huntsville Utilities’ 200,000 customers will be charged this Availability Fee.\n“That was a decision the [City] Council made to offer some relief to those customers who maybe live in a smaller house, maybe on a more fixed income.”\nGherdes said there are resources available for customers having a hard time paying their bill. He said Huntsville Utilities is also open to working with customers directly to figure out a plan that works for their situation.\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to WHNT.com.",
